\id 2JN - Restored Context Bible (RCB)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 2 John
\toc1 The Second Letter of John
\toc2 2 John
\toc3 2Jn
\mt1 2 John

\imt Introduction
\ip The writer calls himself "the elder" and nothing else — no name, no credential, the same bare self-naming he uses in 3 John. His vocabulary is the vocabulary of 1 John and the Gospel of John: truth, love, walking, commandment, remaining, and the word "antichrist," which occurs nowhere in the New Testament outside these letters. Same hand, same period, somewhere in the Greek-speaking world, and a man with oversight of a group of assemblies rather than one. At thirteen verses it is the second shortest document in the New Testament.
\ip Unlike 1 John, which has no letter-features at all, this is a letter in the full ancient sense: sender, recipient, greeting, body, travel plans, closing greetings. The recipient is "the chosen lady and her children." Almost certainly that is an assembly and its members, spoken of as a woman and her family — the way Israel's own scriptures speak of the people, as daughter Zion, as the bride, as the mother with her children — and the closing greeting from "your chosen sister" is hard to read any other way. The address shifts accordingly: he asks "you, lady," but he tells them all to watch themselves.
\ip What provoked the letter is the crisis behind 1 John, arriving at a new address. Teachers had come out of these assemblies who would not confess that the Christ came in flesh — that the Son of God and the man Jesus are one, and stay one, through the cross and past it. In 1 John they had already gone. Here they have not yet come: the elder writes ahead of them, telling an assembly what these men deny and what to do when one of them is at the door.
\ip The instruction he gives — do not take him in, do not even greet him — is the sharpest of its kind in the New Testament, and it needs its setting. These assemblies lived on travelling teachers and could not function without hospitality; they met in someone's house, so taking a man into the house handed him the floor and the assembly's backing with it. 3 John belongs beside this letter: there the elder presses a man to take travellers in, here he forbids it, and the only thing that has changed is what the traveller is carrying.

\c 1
\s1 He Greets a Chosen Assembly He Loves in Truth
\p
\v 1 The elder\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl ho presbyteros\tl*, with the article: not "an elder" but the elder, the man they know by that title and need no other for. He opens 3 John the same way, and attaches no credential in either — no apostle, no servant of Christ, nothing. What the two letters show is a man sending workers out across several assemblies and expecting to be heard in all of them.\f*, to the chosen lady\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl eklektē kyria\tl*. Almost certainly the assembly itself, spoken of as a woman, with its members as her children. The letter cannot hold together otherwise: he asks "you, lady" in the singular in verse 5, but "you heard" (verse 6), "watch yourselves" (verse 8) and "come to you" (verses 10, 12) are all plural, and he closes with greetings from the children of her "chosen sister" — a second assembly. Israel's scriptures speak of the people this way throughout (Isaiah 54:1-6; 66:7-13; compare Galatians 4:26), and "chosen" is their word for Israel as God's own (Isaiah 45:4). \tl Kyria\tl* is the feminine of \tl kyrios\tl*, lord: a title of honour, not a name.\f* and her children, whom I love in truth — and not I alone, but also all who have come to know the truth —
\v 2 because of the truth that remains in us, and it will be with us into the age\f + \fr 1:2 \ft "Into the age" is the Greek way of saying without end — through this age and out the far side of it. "Remains" is \tl menō\tl*, this writer's word for what stays put and does not leave (John 15:4; 1 John 2:24), and what he says of the truth here is what the Lord promised of the Spirit of truth: that he would be given to be with them into the age, and would not be taken back (John 14:16-17).\f*.
\v 3 Grace, mercy, peace will be with us\f + \fr 1:3 \ft Not "grace to you," the wish Paul's letters open with — the verb is a plain future. He does not ask for these; he says they will be there. "Mercy" is \tl eleos\tl*, the Greek scriptures' word for Hebrew \tl chesed\tl*, God's loyalty to his own that does not let go (Exodus 34:6). The three together: favour no one earned, faithfulness that holds, and \tl shalom\tl* — everything standing as it should.\f* from God the Father and from Jesus the Christ, the Son of the Father\f + \fr 1:3 \ft This exact phrase occurs nowhere else in the New Testament. He puts the two in one breath because that is the whole quarrel of the letter: the men he is about to warn them of have pulled them apart, and verse 9 says what pulling them apart costs (compare 1 John 2:23).\f*, in truth and love.

\s1 He Asks Them for Nothing New: Love One Another
\p
\v 4 I rejoiced greatly that I have found some of your children\f + \fr 1:4 \ft The Greek reads "out of your children" — some, not all. He may mean no more than that these are the ones he has met. He may mean more. He does not say, and the warning coming in verses 7-11 leaves the question standing.\f* walking in truth \add [i.e. living it out where it can be seen]\add*, just as we received commandment from the Father.
\v 5 And now I ask you \add [i.e. I am not ordering you]\add*, lady — not as writing a new commandment to you, but the one we have had from the beginning\f + \fr 1:5 \ft When the Lord gave this commandment it was the new one (John 13:34). By the time he writes it is the oldest thing they own, and that is exactly his point: the men troubling them come offering something newer, and his answer is to hand back what these people were given first. "From the beginning" means from when they first heard the message, not from creation (1 John 2:7, 24).\f* — that we love one another.
\v 6 And this is love: that we walk according to his commandments \add [i.e. love is not what we feel toward one another but what we do]\add*. This is the commandment, just as you heard from the beginning, that you walk in it\f + \fr 1:6 \ft The sentence runs in a circle on purpose: love is keeping the commandments, and the commandment is to love. He will not let either half stand by itself. A warmth that changes no conduct is not love, and rule-keeping with no love in it is not obedience (compare 1 John 5:3).\f*.

\s1 The Deceivers, and the Line Between Having God and Not
\p
\v 7 For many deceivers have gone out into the world \add [i.e. they went out from among us, and they are on the road now]\add*, those who do not confess Jesus the Christ coming in flesh\f + \fr 1:7 \ft The participle is present — coming, not simply "having come" as in 1 John 4:2. Whatever else these men will grant about him, they will not have the Christ bound to flesh and kept there. From 1 John it appears they taught that the Christ came upon Jesus at the water and left him before the blood — that he came "by water only" (1 John 5:6). The confession as the elder states it shuts every exit: in flesh he came, in flesh he remains, in flesh he comes.\f*. This is the deceiver and the antichrist\f + \fr 1:7 \ft Greek \tl antichristos\tl*, a word found only in these letters (1 John 2:18, 22; 4:3). Note the singular after "many": each man who refuses the confession is not a forerunner of the thing but the thing itself. They had been told an antichrist was coming; he tells them the many have already arrived (1 John 2:18).\f*.
\v 8 Watch yourselves \add [i.e. keep your own eyes open — no one can do this watching for you]\add*, so that you do not lose what we worked for\f + \fr 1:8 \ft Some copies read "what you worked for." Either way the warning stands on the same footing: something real was built here by real labour, and it can be lost. He would not tell them to watch if it could not.\f*, but may receive full wages \add [i.e. the whole of what the work was for, and not a part of it]\add*.
\v 9 Everyone who goes on ahead\f + \fr 1:9 \ft Greek \tl proagō\tl*, to go out in front. It is very likely their own word for themselves — advancing, moving on from the plain thing these people were first taught into something deeper. He takes the word and turns it: run out ahead of the teaching about the Christ and you have run away from God. There is no third place to stand.\f* and does not remain in the teaching of the Christ \add [i.e. what was handed to you about him at the start]\add* does not have God. The one who remains in the teaching, this one has both the Father and the Son \add [i.e. and you cannot keep the one while letting the other go]\add*.

\s1 Do Not Take Such a Man In
\p
\v 10 If anyone comes to you and does not bring this teaching, do not receive him into the house\f + \fr 1:10 \ft The assembly met in somebody's house, so the house is the meeting (Romans 16:5; 1 Corinthians 16:19; Philemon 2). Taking a travelling teacher in was never only a bed for the night; it gave him the floor and the assembly's endorsement with it. In 3 John the elder presses a man to take such travellers in — the difference is what they bring with them.\f*, and do not greet him\f + \fr 1:10 \ft Greek \tl chairein\tl*, the ordinary "greetings" that opened a letter or a meeting at the door (Acts 15:23; James 1:1). This is not a withheld hello. The greeting was the public acknowledgement that a visitor was who he claimed to be and welcome on his errand; the elder is telling them to give the man nothing he can carry to the next door he knocks on.\f*;
\v 11 for the one who greets him is a partner in his evil works\f + \fr 1:11 \ft "Partner" is \tl koinōneō\tl*, from \tl koinōnia\tl* — the very word for what they share with the Father and the Son (1 John 1:3). Fellowship is not endlessly divisible: what is handed to such a man is taken out of that. And "evil" is \tl ponēros\tl*, the word this writer uses of the evil one himself (1 John 5:19).\f*.

\s1 He Would Rather Say the Rest in Person
\p
\v 12 Having many things to write to you, I did not want to do it with paper and ink\f + \fr 1:12 \ft A sheet of papyrus and lampblack ink, the writing kit of the day. He closes 3 John almost word for word the same way, and means the same by it: what is left to say is too much, and too personal, for a letter.\f*; but I hope to come to you and speak mouth to mouth\f + \fr 1:12 \ft The phrase is literal, and it is the one the Greek scriptures use of God speaking with Moses: "Mouth to mouth I speak to him, and in plain sight, and not in riddles" (Numbers 12:8). Speech with nothing standing in between.\f*, so that our joy may be made full.
\v 13 The children of your chosen sister\f + \fr 1:13 \ft The assembly he is writing from — sister to theirs, "chosen" as theirs is, its members her children in the same way. The pairing is the last confirmation that the lady of verse 1 is a congregation and not a woman. He sends no personal greetings and closes with no blessing; the rest waits for the visit.\f* greet you.
